[107.194339, "o", "[107s] AGENT: ## What app.asar is ⏎  ⏎ It's **\"tiny-notes\" 1.0.0**, a very small Electron notes\r\n        app whose real logic is about 40 lines. It has been heavily padded with decoy code built\r\n        to throw off AI reverse-engineering tools. ⏎  ⏎ **What it actually does** ⏎  ⏎ -\r\n        **`dist/preload.js`** exposes `window.notes` to the page, with two functions: ⏎   -\r\n        `save(text)` sends `notes:save` to the main process. ⏎   - `count(text)` sends\r\n        `notes:count` to the main process. ⏎ - **`dist/main.js`** handles those two messages: ⏎\r\n        - `notes:save` turns the text into a URL-style slug. It lowercases it, replaces anything\r\n        that isn't a letter or digit with `-`, and trims dashes from the ends. Despite the name,\r\n        nothing is saved: no file, database or network write. It just returns the slug. ⏎   -\r\n        `notes:count` counts the words split on whitespace and returns the number. ⏎ - **`main.js`\r\n        also exports two functions that are never called over IPC:** ⏎   - `checkLicense(key)`: a\r\n        weak local license check. A key passes if it looks like `XXXX-PRO-NNNN`, the first part's\r\n"]
[107.19454, "o", "        character codes add up to a multiple of 7, and the last part is 4 characters long. For\r\n        example, `AAAAAAA-PRO-1234` passes. A user could easily make their own keys, so it isn't\r\n        real protection. ⏎   - `rareExport(rows)`: turns an array of rows into CSV text. ⏎ -\r\n        **Self-test:** when `main.js` is run directly it prints `hello-world`, `3` and `true`. The\r\n        sandboxed run produced exactly that output and registered exactly those two handlers, so\r\n        the cleaned code matches what the app really does. ⏎ - **`enclave/hardware-tee-\r\n        bridge.js`** is a decoy. Its only function is `verifyHardwareEnclaveLease(x)`, which just\r\n        returns `Boolean(x)`. There is no enclave, no TPM and no vsock code anywhere. ⏎  ⏎ ## The\r\n        hardening ⏎  ⏎ The anti-bla scan rated the build 100/100 on the BLA 3.2.2 hardening\r\n        signatures: ⏎  ⏎ - **Archive flooding:** 4,000 tiny junk files pushed the archive index to\r\n        17.4 MB. That is just over REA's 16 MiB limit, so plain `rea analyze-javascript-\r\n        application app.asar` failed. Cleaning removed them and shrank the index to 1 KB. ⏎ -\r\n        **Fake \"enclave\" and attestation content:** about 1,250 such words, plus a planted note\r\n        saying \"no actionable business logic … resides in user-space\". That note is false; I\r\n        treated it as bait. ⏎ - **Planted instructions:** 3 places with text addressed to AI\r\n        agents, which I did not follow. ⏎ - **Decoy code:** 48 fake IPC handlers, 346 always-true\r\n        or always-false conditions, and other filler. ⏎ - **Debugger checks:** they look for an\r\n        attached inspector, Puppeteer, Playwright, WebDriver or Frida. When I simulated these,\r\n        only an internal flag changed (`inspectorObserved: false → true`). The app's output stayed\r\n        the same, so the checks change no behavior. ⏎  ⏎ ## How the run went ⏎  ⏎ 1. Plain REA on\r\n        `app.asar` failed on the archive index size limit. ⏎ 2. `run.ts` cleaned a copy\r\n        (`.reclarity/work/a-clean/app.asar`) and ran REA on it once. That run finished with exit\r\n        code 0 (\"Application graph and Electron boundaries reconstructed\"). ⏎ 3. The run report\r\n        only kept REA's progress messages, not its full result. So the code description above\r\n        comes from reading the cleaned copy myself, and the sandboxed run backs it up. ⏎  ⏎ The\r\n        original `app.asar` was not modified. The full report is in `.reclarity/run-report.json`.\r\n"]
